title: Turn-based play
intro: Sides that take turns, with no code, while the rest of the game runs in real time.
project: Platformer | Taking Turns

chapter: A turn order | Entity, Gameplay, Turn Order
deselect
create: Turn Order
explain: menu:Entity | Entity, Gameplay, Turn Order | The Entity menu makes a game turn-based: Turn Order, under Gameplay.
explain: component:TurnOrder | Turn Order | It holds the turns. Its text in the corner says whose turn it is, and it comes with an End Turn button.
explain: field:TurnOrder.Sides | Sides | The sides take turns in this order: the player, then the enemy. Two people at one keyboard could be blue and red.
explain: field:TurnOrder.ComputerSides | Computer Sides | The computer plays the enemy, so its turn ends by itself, after its Computer Turn Time.
set: TurnOrder.ComputerTurnTime = 2

chapter: Who takes turns | A Turn Taker on each side
select: Player
browse: TurnTaker
explain: catalog:docs | Turn Taker | Whatever acts gets a Turn Taker, naming its side.
click: catalog:add
explain: component:TurnTaker | Player | The player is on the Player side.
select: Slime
browse: TurnTaker
click: catalog:add
set: TurnTaker.Side = Enemy
explain: field:TurnTaker.Side | Enemy | And the slime is on the Enemy side. Off its turn, its patrol holds still.
play
wait: 0.5
hold: Right 1
explain: game | The player's turn | It's the player's turn: they run about, and the slime waits on its ledge.
tap: Tab
wait: 0.5
expectvalue: Turns.TurnOrder.Current = Enemy
hold: Left 1
explain: game | The enemy's turn | Tab ends the turn. Now the slime walks, and the player's keys do nothing. Everything else, the lift and the coins, runs in real time as ever.
wait: 1
expectvalue: Turns.TurnOrder.Current = Player
explain: game | Round two | Two seconds on, the computer's turn is over, and it's the player's again.
stop

chapter: Actions | One swing a turn
select: Player
set: TurnTaker.Actions = 1
explain: field:TurnTaker.Actions | Actions | Actions limit what a turn taker does in a turn. Each sword swing or shot uses one. With one, a swing ends the player's turn.
play
wait: 0.5
tap: X
wait: 1
expectvalue: Turns.TurnOrder.Current = Enemy
explain: game | Swung | The player swung, used their only action, and after a moment the turn passed to the slime by itself.
stop
say: That's turn-based play with no code. Scripts can end turns and use actions too, rewinding goes back to whose turn it was, and in network games each team takes its turn.
